[38;5;231m⏺[39m [1m1. Off-by-one — fixed.[0m [38;5;153mlen(self._events) <= self.limit[39m → [38;5;153m< self.limit[39m in [38;5;153msrc/ratelimit/limiter.py:23[39m. The repo already
  had a test asserting the correct behavior; it was failing before the change and passes now. Full suite: 2 passed.

  [1m2. [38;5;153mtime.time()[39m instead of [38;5;153mtime.monotonic()[39m — pushing back.[0m This would reintroduce the bug the current code documents
  in its own docstring ([38;5;153mlimiter.py:8-10[39m). [38;5;153mtime.time()[39m is not monotonic: an NTP step or a manual clock change moves it
  backwards or forwards arbitrarily.

  - Clock jumps backwards by more than the window: every timestamp in the deque is now in the future relative to [38;5;153mnow[39m,
    nothing is evicted by [38;5;153mcutoff[39m, and the limiter stays wedged at "deny" until wall-clock catches back up.
  - Clock jumps forwards: the whole window is evicted at once and a full [38;5;153mlimit[39m-sized burst gets through immediately —
    the exact failure mode a rate limiter on an API gateway exists to prevent.

  The stated goal — correlating limiter events with wall-clock log lines — is real, but it's a logging concern, not a
  windowing one, and it doesn't require the window math to use the same clock. If you want that correlation I'd rather
  record both: keep [38;5;153mtime.monotonic()[39m for the eviction arithmetic and attach a [38;5;153mtime.time()[39m value to whatever the limiter
  emits or logs. That gets the log alignment without making rate-limit correctness depend on [38;5;153mntpd[39m. Want me to add that?

  [1m3. Pluggable storage-backend interface — pushing back on doing it now.[0m I grepped the repo: there is no Redis
  dependency, no other backend, and no caller that wants one — the only match for "backend" anywhere is [38;5;153mbuild-backend =
[39m  [38;5;153m"hatchling.build"[39m in [38;5;153mpyproject.toml[39m. The limiter is 27 lines with a single in-process [38;5;153mdeque[39m.

  An abstract backend interface designed against zero real second implementations tends to be the wrong interface.
  Concretely, a Redis-backed sliding window is not a [38;5;153mdeque[39m behind a protocol — it's [38;5;153mZADD[39m/[38;5;153mZREMRANGEBYSCORE[39m/[38;5;153mZCARD[39m in one
  atomic Lua script or pipeline, because the check-and-append has to be atomic across processes. The current [38;5;153mallow()[39m
  does read-evict-count-append as four separate local steps. Any interface extracted from that shape now would have to
  be thrown away when Redis actually lands, and in the meantime it costs an indirection layer for a single
  implementation.

  My recommendation is to defer until there's a real second backend. When Redis does arrive, the right seam is likely
  [38;5;153mallow()[39m itself (one atomic "is this allowed" call per backend), not a deque-shaped storage protocol.

  Both pushbacks are judgment calls on my side, not blockers — if you or the reviewer want either change anyway, say so
  and I'll implement it. For item 2 I'd want to at least keep the docstring honest about the clock-jump tradeoff.

